\section{Parsing prose}{

Beyond plain and lightly-formatted dates, \code{as_messydate()} recognises
several conventions common in e.g. historical texts and converts them to
their ISO 8601-2 equivalent before the usual parsing takes place:
\itemize{
\item{Roman numerals for a bare year, e.g. \code{"MDCCLXXVI"} becomes \code{1776}.}
\item{Roman calendar references, i.e. the Kalends, Nones, and Ides of a
named month, e.g. \code{"the Ides of March, 44 BC"} becomes \code{"-0043-03-15"}.
The Nones and Ides fall later (the 7th and 15th) in March, May, July,
and October, and earlier (the 5th and 13th) in other months.}
\item{Approximate qualifiers ("around", "circa", "about", "roughly", ...)
add the \code{~} annotation, and uncertain qualifiers ("possibly", "perhaps",
"reportedly", ...) add \verb{?}; both together add \verb{\%}, e.g.
\code{"possibly about 1910"} becomes \code{"\%1910"}.}
\item{Connectives joining two days of the same month: "between the 13th
and 15th" or "from the 13th to the 15th" become a range (\code{..}); "the
13th or the 15th" becomes a set (\code{{}}); and a plain "the 13th and the
15th" becomes two separate dates.}
\item{"before"/"prior to"/"no later than" and "after"/"since"/"no
earlier than" become an open range, e.g. \code{"before 1910"} becomes
\code{"..1910"}. The bound may itself be any precision the parser
understands, including a decade or century.}
\item{Decades ("the 1920s" becomes \code{"192X"}) and centuries ("the 19th
century" becomes \code{"18XX"}).}
\item{A day of the year, e.g. \code{"the 103rd day of 2026"} becomes
\code{"2026-04-13"}. The ISO 8601-2 notation for this (\code{"2026-103"}) is
rejected instead of read, since it is easily read as a mistake for
\code{"2026-10-03"}.}
\item{A week of the year, e.g. \code{"the 5th week of 2026"} becomes the range
\code{"2026-01-26..2026-02-01"}. Weeks follow the ISO 8601 rule: a week starts
on a Monday, and week 1 is the week holding the first Thursday of the year,
so week 1 can start in December of the previous year. The ISO 8601-2 week
date is read too, as \code{"2026-W05"} or, for one day within the week,
\code{"2026-W05-3"}, but it is never written back in that notation.}
\item{Seasons, e.g. \code{"summer 2026"} becomes the month range
\code{"2026-06..2026-08"}. These are the northern-hemisphere meteorological
seasons, spring running from March to May and winter from December into
February of the following year. The EDTF season codes (\code{"2026-22"}) are
rejected instead of read, being easily read as a mistake for a date.}
\item{A comma-separated list of dates in prose, e.g.
\code{"13th Feb, 1977, Feb 15 1977, 1910"}, is split into separate dates
(here, three): a fragment that is only a year is treated as completing
the date before it.}
}
}

\section{Eras and year numbering}{

\code{{messydates}} stores years in the ISO 8601-2 (proleptic Gregorian)
astronomical numbering, in which a year zero exists and equals 1 BCE, \code{-0001}
equals 2 BCE, and so on. Historical "BC"/"BCE" prose uses the older
convention that has no year zero, so it is converted on input: a historical
year \verb{N BCE} becomes the astronomical year \code{-(N-1)}, e.g. \code{"1 BCE"} becomes
\code{"0000"}, \code{"2 BCE"} becomes \code{"-0001"}, and \code{"44 BCE"} becomes \code{"-0043"}.
A year written directly in signed ISO form (e.g. \code{"-0044"}) is already
astronomical and is left unchanged, so \code{as_messydate("-0044")} (astronomical
year -44, i.e. 45 BCE) and \code{as_messydate("44 BCE")} (\code{"-0043"}) intentionally
differ. "AD"/"CE" prose is simply dropped, the year being unchanged.

Era prose is a matter of input only: it is always removed on parsing, the
era of a date in an \code{mdate} being carried by the sign of its year alone.
Where it appears in the input, it is resolved for each year separately. A
marker written before a date governs that date (\code{"{BC1044-03-15,BC1033}"});
otherwise a year takes the era of the first marker written after it, so a
marker given once at the end still applies to every bound of a range or set:
\code{"200..100 BC"}, \code{"..200 BC"} and \code{"200 BC.."} are all wholly BCE, whereas
\code{"200 BC..100 AD"} spans the two eras and gives \code{"-0199..0100"}.
}

